 ## Background
-The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting optimization loop was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S2, unofficial]. It established the baseline for differentiable rendering with 3D Gaussians, utilizing a composite loss of L1 and D-SSIM, Adam optimization, and heuristic-based densification and pruning. Subsequent methods have extended this loop to address specific challenges such as training speed ([TurboGS](15697-turbogs.md), [Frequency Guided Resolution Scheduler](15751-frequency-guided-resolution-scheduler.md)), memory efficiency ([Sparse SH Order Increment](18689-sparse-sh-order-increment.md)), sparse view reconstruction ([Active View Selection and Residual Learning for 3DGS](20899-active-view-selection-and-residual-learning-for-3dgs.md)), and integration with other modalities like diffusion models ([Gaussian Splatting Training with SDS Loss](18710-gaussian-splatting-training-with-sds-loss.md)) or CAD models ([Geometric Initialization](22461-gradient-modification.md)).
+The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting optimization loop was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S2, unofficial]. It relies on a differentiable rasterizer to render images from 3D Gaussians and minimizes a composite loss of L1 and D-SSIM using the Adam optimizer. Subsequent methods have extended this foundation to address specific challenges such as training speed [TurboGS](15697-turbogs.md), memory efficiency [Sparse SH Order Increment](18689-sparse-sh-order-increment.md), sparse view reconstruction [Active View Selection](20899-active-view-selection-and-residual-learning-for-3dgs.md), and integration with other modalities like diffusion models [SDS Loss](18710-gaussian-splatting-training-with-sds-loss.md) or CAD models [Geometric Initialization](22286-3d-gaussian-splatting-optimization.md).
 
